# Source-worked cases retain their actual course data and worked source content.
# These descriptions are explicit to the particular case, not generated by
# padding a short prompt with a universal instruction paragraph.
source_contexts={
'language':['A population is the collection of individuals or possible measurements to which a biomedical conclusion is intended to apply. Investigators usually obtain a finite random sample from that population and use its recorded values to estimate the population’s properties.','A report distinguishes the uncertain response before an individual is measured, the value actually recorded, the average calculated from one sample and the fixed population mean. It also distinguishes sample variability from population variability. Identify the notation representing each quantity before carrying out statistical calculations.'],
'classify':['A biomedical data set combines measurements from clinical instruments, counts of recorded events and patient classification variables. An analyst needs to identify the scale represented by each entry before selecting descriptive summaries.','Classify height, LDL concentration, blood pressure, number of tender joints, age recorded in whole years, ZIP code, country of birth, cancer stage and pain category using the four data classes taught in the course. The classification concerns the recorded variable, including any rounding, rather than an idealized measurement that was not recorded.'],
'describe':['Seventy BME freshmen form one population of interest. A random sample of ten freshmen is selected, and the measured heights in inches are listed below. Investigators want to use the sample to estimate the center and variability of the freshman population and characterize uncertainty in the estimated population mean.','Use the recorded observations as a sample. Show the deviations or the equivalent squared-sum calculation used for the variance, and distinguish the sample SD from the standard error used to describe the reliability of the estimated mean.'],
'percent':['A random sample of ten BME seniors has the heights in inches shown below in ascending order. The researchers want percentile and spread summaries for the sample as part of a comparison of freshman and senior height populations.','Use the course’s rank convention when locating a requested percentile. A fractional rank identifies a position between recorded observations; the answer must be expressed as a height, not as the rank itself. The same ordered sample is used for the median, first quartile, interquartile range and total range.'],
'discrete':['An experiment is described by a discrete random variable X. Its probability function is shown below and is zero at all other values. The possible values are numerical response values, so the expectation is obtained by weighting each response by its probability.','Determine whether the function can describe a valid random variable and use it to calculate both the center and dispersion. The mathematical report must describe the full distribution, including outcomes outside the support.'],
'depend':['Consider two experiments used to study independent and dependent outcomes. In the first experiment, a fair coin is tossed three times, with independent tosses. In the second experiment, two cards are selected sequentially from a standard deck containing four kings among 52 cards, without replacing the first card.','The outcomes of interest are exactly two heads in the coin experiment and a king on both draws in the card experiment. Build the probabilities from the possible outcomes and the relevant conditional probabilities. In particular, account for what remains in the deck after the first king is drawn.'],
'continuous':['The time required by a procedure is modeled by the continuous density f(x)=x/2 for values from zero to two, with density zero elsewhere. Investigators want to describe the distribution of a processing time and determine the frequency of times in specified ranges.','Use the defining integral relationships for densities, distribution functions and moments. Include the portions of the CDF outside the support. Calculate point and interval probabilities using the continuous model, rather than treating the density height as the probability of a single time.'],
'endpoint':['A proposed probability model for a continuous experimental response has density f(x)=.1+x for 0<x≤c and zero elsewhere. The positive endpoint c has not yet been determined. The shape is specified, but the support must be chosen so that the total probability is one.','Determine the admissible endpoint from the defining density properties. Then obtain the distribution function on the support, retaining the correct integration limits. Any candidate root that contradicts the stated positive support is not an admissible endpoint.'],
'normal':['A physiological measurement is modeled as Normal with population mean μ and population SD σ. The laboratory needs a mathematical description of both the density and cumulative probabilities so that an observed measurement can be placed relative to its population.','Use the course’s Normal parameter convention, in which the second parameter in N(μ,σ²) is the variance. Relate an arbitrary measurement to the standard Normal and identify how density symmetry and cumulative left areas give probabilities below, above and between measurement cutoffs.'],
'normalwork':['A health-screening program assumes that fasting blood-glucose concentrations in healthy adults aged 30–50 are Normally distributed with mean 95 mg/dL and SD 8.7 mg/dL. The response X is the glucose concentration of one randomly selected individual.','Use the standard Normal table to describe how much of this population lies below 85 mg/dL, between 85 and 115 mg/dL, and above 115 mg/dL. The reported calculations must show the standardization and the cumulative areas read from the table. Also distinguish the population variance from its stated SD and the probability at an exact concentration from a density height.'],
'inverse':['The fasting blood-glucose concentration of healthy adults in the study population is Normally distributed with mean 95 mg/dL and SD 8.7 mg/dL. A report needs measurement cutoffs corresponding to specified positions within the population, rather than probabilities for already specified measurements.','Find the lower and upper quartiles and the 97.5th percentile. Use cumulative areas to obtain standard Normal cutoffs and then return those cutoffs to the original mg/dL scale. Show the conversion and retain the concentration units in the final answers.'],
'sampling':['An impurity measurement has population mean 4 g and population SD 1.5 g. An investigator selects 50 independent observations and calculates their sample average. The question concerns whether this reported average lies between 3.5 and 3.8 g, rather than whether an individual impurity measurement lies in that range.','Use the course’s sampling-distribution approximation for the average of a sufficiently large independent sample. Identify the distribution’s center and spread before standardizing the endpoints, and use the Normal table to obtain the requested interval probability.'],
'linear':['A combined biomedical response is formed as aX+bY+c from two independent random variables with known population means and variances. The coefficients change the scale of the measurements, while the additive constant changes the reported origin.','Determine the mean and variance of the combined response in terms of the supplied parameters. Then relate these properties to repeated sample averages: determine the change in SEM when sample size is multiplied by four, and identify the course’s standard-error expression for a sample variance.'],
'pooled':['A clinical investigation compares glucose concentration in two independent groups. The first group has mean 82.2, sample SD 2.49 and 25 subjects; the second has mean 86.1, sample SD 2.09 and 25 subjects. The measurements are interval data.','The investigators ask whether there is a difference between the two glucose population means. Assume Normal populations with equal population variances. Use the test specific to two independent groups and a two-sided significance level .05; define the signed difference as Group 1 minus Group 2.'],
'unequal':['A cardiac investigation compares cardiac index in two independent patient groups. Group 1 contains nine patients, with sample mean 2.08 and sample SD 1.05. Group 2 contains sixteen patients, with sample mean 1.75 and sample SD .88.','Determine whether the two cardiac-index population means differ at α=.05. Assume Normal populations with a common variance. The unequal sample sizes must be retained in the pooled variance and standard error; this is not an equal-size shortcut problem.'],
'onet':['A random sample of five individuals has mean heart rate 76.8 BPM and sample SD 4.15 BPM. A reference population has mean heart rate 72 BPM. Investigators want to determine whether the study population mean differs from that reference.','Assume a Normal distribution in the study population and use a two-sided significance level .05. Treat the reference value as fixed, without creating a second sample variance or second sample size. The conclusion must concern the population mean heart rate, rather than merely observe that 76.8 differs numerically from 72.'],
'anova':['A xenograft experiment compares three dosing regimens of an isotype-control antibody. Twelve mice are studied in each group, and final tumor volume is measured 29 days after implantation. The group means and SDs in cubic millimeters are given below.','Determine whether there is a difference among the tumor-volume population means at α=.05. Use the balanced-study variance method taught in the course, assuming independent observations, Normal populations and a common population variance. If a difference is found, do not test which groups differ.'],
'pairs':['An HDL study compares three treatment groups containing 70 subjects each. Their sample means are 64.8, 58.0 and 43.3. The original ANOVA gives within variance 239.9 and residual df 207. Follow-up comparisons concern which treatment population means differ.','Use the original ANOVA variance estimate and degrees of freedom for the pair statistics. Determine the requested family before adjusting comparisons: all three pairs form one possible family, while the two active treatments compared only against the inactive control form another.'],
'multiplicity':['An HDL treatment study has three requested all-pair comparisons after an overall mean difference. All comparisons use the same original ANOVA variance and residual df 207. Their statistic magnitudes are approximately 8.2, 5.6 and 2.6.','Use overall significance level .05. Apply the course’s multiplicity procedures to the requested all-pair family, retaining labels when ordering results. Distinguish the total two-sided significance level used in the supplied table from the cumulative area used for an inverse-t calculation.'],
'z':['Patients receiving placebo or aspirin are followed for thrombus formation. The placebo group has 18 thrombi among 25 patients, while the aspirin group has six among nineteen patients. Each person contributes a binary outcome, and the complete observed counts are shown below.','Determine whether the two treatment population event proportions differ at a two-sided level .05. Use the course’s z procedure with its continuity correction. Pool actual event counts over actual group totals under the population null; do not average two percentages without considering their different group sizes.'],
'chi2':['Patients receiving placebo or aspirin are followed for thrombus formation. The observed rows below classify each patient as thrombus or no thrombus, with 25 placebo patients and nineteen aspirin patients. The counts describe a two-group, two-outcome population comparison.','At α=.05, determine whether the thrombus outcome distribution differs between the two treatment populations. Use the course’s two-by-two correction. Show how the observed margins determine expected counts, and retain both the event and no-event columns when calculating the statistic.'],
'large':['A depression study compares three independent treatment groups and records one of two outcomes for each patient. The observed table is shown below. The investigator wants to know whether all three treatment populations have the same outcome distribution.','Perform the overall nominal-outcome comparison at α=.05. The table is three by two, so the overall comparison uses ordinary cell contributions. If follow-up pairs are considered, distinguish that larger-table calculation from the corrected two-by-two comparisons and their required multiplicity adjustment.'],
'gof':['A health study classifies a sample of 470 people into three exercise categories. The observed counts are 255, 125 and 90, while a proposed population pattern assigns proportions .60, .25 and .15. Every person appears in exactly one category.','Determine at α=.05 whether the actual population pattern differs from the stated proportions. Use the test appropriate to one observed category list compared with fixed target probabilities, without estimating additional parameters for those targets from the sample.'],
'risk':['In a prospective thrombus study, six of nineteen aspirin patients and eighteen of twenty-five placebo patients experience the event. The groups are followed from treatment to outcome, so the full group denominators are available. Aspirin is the active treatment and placebo is the reference.','In a separate case-control study, investigators sample disease cases and controls and then ascertain previous exposure. The case sample contains fifty exposed and fourteen unexposed people; the control sample contains forty-three exposed and thirty-five unexposed people. Select the association measure appropriate to each sampling design and retain the requested comparison direction.'],
'output':['Patients with myocardial infarctions are assigned to standard care or to one of three active thrombolytic regimens and followed for mortality. The homework supplies all six pairwise corrected chi-square results, but the scientific request is to identify which active treatments differ from standard care.','Only the three active-versus-control results belong to the requested family. They are 18.234 for Group 3 versus control, 9.4944 for Group 1 versus control and 4.6895 for Group 2 versus control, each with one df. Use overall significance level .05, retain the treatment labels and apply Holm’s sequential procedure.'],
'proportionwork':['A clinical group exposed to halothane contains 61 patients, of whom eight die. Death is the event of interest and the binary response equals one for death and zero otherwise. The investigator wants to estimate the event probability in the corresponding patient population.','Calculate both the variation among individual binary responses and the uncertainty in the estimated event fraction. These use the same event proportion but answer different questions, so identify which denominator and square-root operation belong to the requested standard error.'],
'anovaoutput':['A biomedical study compares nitric-oxide measurements across treatment groups. A statistical analyst supplies an ANOVA table, with the Group-row p value .0106. The printed mean-square entries are rounded, while the reported statistic is obtained from the full-precision analysis.','Use the supplied output to decide whether the treatment population means differ at α=.05. If follow-up population-mean comparisons are requested, identify the appropriate noise variance and degrees of freedom from the same analysis, rather than calculating new two-group residual degrees of freedom.'],
'functionmean':['A continuous experimental response has density .1+x for 0<x≤c, with the positive endpoint chosen to give total probability one. A transformed response is defined as 2X+1, and its average is needed for the report.','A separate Normal glucose variable has mean 95 mg/dL and SD 8.7 mg/dL, and a new reporting scale subtracts 26 mg/dL from each glucose measurement. Determine the expected values and variability of the transformed responses using the course rules for functions and linear changes.'],
'cltwhy':['Independent observations have a common population mean μ and variance σ². Investigators calculate the mean of n observations and repeat the sampling procedure to study the reliability of that mean. The average is itself a random variable whose value changes from sample to sample.','Use the expected-value and variance rules for independent sums to derive the center and spread of this sampling distribution. Distinguish the exact Normal case from the course’s large-sample approximation when individual observations do not have a Normal population distribution.'],
}
