source_contexts['binary']=['A group of n independently observed patients contains m instances of an event. For a patient, X=1 denotes the event and X=0 denotes no event. The unknown population event proportion is estimated using this sample.', 'The report needs the estimated event fraction, the variance and SD of individual binary outcomes, and the standard error of the estimated fraction. Write the appropriate course expressions and explain how the role of sample size differs between individual variation and uncertainty in an estimated proportion.']
source_contexts['uniform']=['A laboratory processing time is Uniform on a bounded interval from a to b. The model must describe the full cumulative probability, not only values inside the interval. A standard Uniform example takes a=0 and b=1.', 'Write the general density and CDF, then use integration for the standard case to calculate the mean, second moment and variance. Interpret an interval probability as the area within the supported range and distinguish that area from the constant density height.']
source_contexts['uniform-definition']=source_contexts['uniform']
source_contexts['uniform-worked']=['A specimen-processing time, expressed on a normalized time scale, is uniformly distributed between zero and one. The density is one within that interval and zero elsewhere, and the CDF must describe probability below every possible cutoff.', 'Use the defining integrals for the CDF and moments. Show the integration limits and antiderivatives used for the mean and second moment, then obtain the variance from their relationship. Relate the resulting CDF to the steadily accumulated area under the constant density.']
source_contexts['trial']=['A clinical investigation can assign a treatment at enrollment, follow people who already have different exposures, or select participants based on disease status. These approaches produce different forms of information about populations.', 'Describe the distinctions among randomized trials, prospective cohorts and case-control studies. Then identify what randomization, masking, sham control, parallel groups and crossover accomplish. Apply each term to what happens to the participants or treatment information, rather than treating all design controls as the same procedure.']
source_contexts['decision']=['A clinical investigator wants to determine whether a measured population response differs from a reference or among treatments. A sample can show numerical differences even when the population null is true, so the observed difference must be evaluated relative to its sampling variation.', 'For the course’s mean-comparison tests, identify the population null, the correct statistic and its reference distribution, degrees of freedom, table tail and decision. State the assumptions under which the comparison is made and express the conclusion about population means, rather than only describing the sample averages.']
source_contexts['chooser']=['Several biomedical studies address different questions. One compares a sample mean with a fixed reference; another compares two independent means; a third compares several equal-size treatment groups. Other studies record binary events, larger tables of categories, or one count list compared with specified population proportions.', 'Identify the course procedure for each question from the number of populations, the outcome scale and the sampling design. A study seeking an association measure must also distinguish prospective event risks from the exposure odds available after sampling by disease status.']
source_contexts['tables']=['A set of hand-calculated biomedical comparisons requires the course’s supplied Normal, t, F and chi-square tables. The tables report different kinds of areas and use different degrees-of-freedom conventions, so the lookup must match the specified procedure.', 'Find the standard Normal cumulative area at z=.05, the two-sided .05 t cutoff with four df and its infinite-df limit, and upper-tail .05 chi-square cutoffs for one and two df. Explain where both F degrees of freedom are located, how its .05/.01 entries differ and how an unavailable df row is handled.']
source_contexts['ti']=['A calculator is used to check several completed hand-worked clinical calculations. The Normal glucose population has mean 95 and SD 8.7; the impurity population has mean 4 and SD 1.5 with a sample average based on fifty independent observations.', 'For glucose, find the interval probability between 85 and 115 and the 97.5th percentile. For the impurity mean, find the probability between 3.5 and 3.8. Then check the one-sample heart-rate and two-sample glucose comparisons using the summaries shown with their solutions. Match each entered spread to an individual measurement or an average, as required by the question.']
