# All multiple-choice items retain the canonical options and correct index.
# Numeric items use the complete linked case; conceptual items receive a study
# vignette plus a specific written justification, not an unexplained short stem.
concept_context={
'classification':'A clinical data set combines instrument readings, event counts and patient categories. An analyst must classify each recorded variable before choosing how to summarize or compare it. The classification must describe the information represented by the entry, including any rounding of the recorded value.',
'summary':'A random sample of physiological measurements is used to describe individual variation and estimate a population mean. The report distinguishes the mean, variance, SD and SEM. Each quantity has a different role in describing the observed sample or the reliability of a population estimate.',
'percentile':'A sample of measurements is used to locate low, central and high observations in an ordered distribution. The course percentile convention determines a rank, after which the result must be read or interpolated on the original measurement scale. The rank itself is not a measurement value.',
'plots':'An investigator examines graphical summaries of a clinical sample before describing its distribution. The display should distinguish location and spread from distribution shape and from agreement with a Normal reference. Interpret what is actually represented by the requested display.',
'random':'An experiment is repeated under a stated probability model. Before the outcome is observed, possible outcomes and their probabilities describe uncertainty. Previous observations affect a future probability only if the model gives a dependence between the relevant events.',
'independent':'Two observations or draws are made sequentially. Whether the first outcome changes the next probability depends on the experimental conditions. A conditional probability must be formed from what is available after the first observation, not assumed equal to the unconditional probability without checking independence.',
'pmf':'A discrete random variable is specified through numerical outcomes and their probability masses. The proposed function must satisfy the probability-function properties across its entire support before its expectation can be used as a description of a valid experiment.',
'dcdf':'A discrete clinical response has probability mass at separated numerical outcomes. The report must determine which function should be used for probabilities at a specified outcome and probabilities below a specified cutoff. Decide how the supported values and boundary conventions enter the requested calculation.',
'expect':'An experimental response has a specified probability function. The report requires expectations of both the response and a function of that response. The function must be applied to possible response values before the resulting values are averaged with their probabilities.',
'variance':'A probability model supplies the mean and second moment of a measurement. The analyst must calculate a measure of dispersion and verify that the selected relationship uses the given moments correctly. A proposed shortcut must agree with the variance definition used in the course.',
'linear':'Two independently measured responses are combined or reported on a changed scale. The resulting report needs a population mean and a measure of variability for the transformed response. Determine the relevant relationship from the coefficients and the independence statement.',
'density':'A biomedical measurement is modeled continuously. A clinician asks how likely a specified measurement or measurement interval is under the model. Another analyst examines whether a proposed density has the properties needed to describe a valid continuous population.',
'uniform':'A processing time is modeled as Uniform over a specified bounded range. The density is constant within the support and zero outside it. An interval probability is obtained from the supported area and must account for the range of times that the model allows.',
'integrate':'A proposed continuous probability density contains an unknown coefficient or support endpoint. The model is not yet fully specified, so its defining probability conditions must be used before moments and interval probabilities can be calculated.',
'ccdf':'A continuous measurement has density f and CDF F. The study asks for tail probabilities, probabilities between two cutoffs or a location dividing the population into two specified fractions. Each request must be translated into the corresponding cumulative-probability statement.',
'cexpect':'A population is described by a continuous density over a specified support. Its mean and other expectations are calculated by integrating a transformed value against that density. Use the actual support in the limits and distinguish the response from the density itself.',
'normal':'A clinical population is described by a Normal model with a stated mean and variability. Standardization relates an individual measurement to the standard Normal reference, and the report must correctly identify the meanings of the stated parameters and the direction of a standardized measurement.',
'normalprob':'A Normal population and measurement cutoffs are specified. The supplied standard Normal table reports cumulative left areas. The requested event must be expressed using the correct left area, complement or difference of two cumulative areas.',
'normalquant':'A clinical report specifies a cumulative fraction of a Normal population and asks for its measurement cutoff. This reverses the direction of a cumulative-probability calculation: an area is given and a location on the original measurement scale is required.',
'sampling':'Independent observations are repeatedly sampled from a population and averaged. The sample average is a random variable that changes from one sample to another. Its center, variability and distributional shape must be distinguished from those of the individual observations.',
'estimators':'An investigator evaluates how reliably a sample statistic estimates a population parameter. Repeated samples produce different estimates, and increasing sample size changes their variation. Use the course’s distinction between the center of an estimator’s distribution and its precision.',
'distributions':'A clinical hypothesis test compares an observed statistic with an appropriate reference distribution. The distribution and tail must match the procedure and its degrees of freedom. A statistic’s direction of extremeness depends on whether the comparison is two-sided or uses an upper tail.',
'choose':'A clinical study provides measurements from one or more populations. Before any calculation, the investigator must identify the outcome scale, number of groups, sampling design and variance assumptions, then select the statistical method that answers the population question.',
'hypothesis':'A study evaluates a population null hypothesis using sample data. The observed statistic is compared with what the null model permits, at a specified significance level. Interpret the evidence and the resulting decision without equating a sample difference or a nonsignificant result with certainty about a population.',
'anova':'Several independent treatment groups have equal sample sizes and provide interval responses. Assume Normal populations with a common variance. The investigator asks whether the treatment population means are all the same and uses the balanced variance method taught in the course.',
'pooledt':'Two independent clinical groups provide interval measurements. Assume Normal population distributions and a common population variance. The study asks whether the two population means differ and uses the course’s pooled two-sample comparison.',
'onet':'A random sample provides an interval response and a fixed reference population mean is supplied. Assume the study population is Normal. The question is whether its population mean differs from the reference, without introducing a second sample or a second estimated variance.',
'posthoc':'An overall ANOVA has established a difference among treatment population means. Selected follow-up comparisons are requested. The variance estimate, residual df and definition of the requested comparison family must be retained from the original analysis.',
'bonferroni':'A clinical study requests several pairwise population comparisons as one family. The overall significance level is .05. Use the requested family, rather than automatically counting every possible pair, and distinguish a two-sided comparison level from the area in one tail.',
'holm':'A study supplies results for a family of treatment comparisons. The treatment labels remain attached to their results when ordering them for the sequential adjustment. Use the changing significance levels and the stopping rule to determine which population nulls are rejected.',
'trials':'A clinical trial must specify how participants receive treatment, what treatment information is concealed and how outcomes are measured. These are distinct design features, and their purposes must be described in terms of what happens to participants or investigators.',
'binary':'Patients are independently followed for an event. An individual response is one if the event occurs and zero otherwise. The estimated event fraction, individual binary variability and uncertainty in the estimated fraction describe different aspects of the same data.',
'zprop':'Two independent clinical groups are followed for a binary event. The population null equates their event probabilities, and the course’s comparison uses a pooled estimate and the stated continuity correction. Group sample sizes and actual event counts must remain attached to their labels.',
'yates':'The same two-group binary data can be analyzed using corrected z or corrected two-by-two chi-square procedures. Apply the course continuity-correction convention at the correct stage of the calculation and retain the relationship between the two statistics.',
'contingency':'A clinical study records counts in a table crossing group membership with outcome category. Under the common-distribution null, the observed margins determine each expected count. Determine the expected-count rule and the properties of its resulting values from the population null, rather than treating an expected count as an additional observation.',
'chi':'A table records outcomes in several clinical groups. The investigator first evaluates whether the population outcome distributions are the same, then may request selected group pairs. The overall table dimensions determine the initial df; each follow-up two-by-two table has its own corrected statistic and multiplicity requirement.',
'gof':'An observed sample is classified into mutually exclusive categories and compared with a fully specified population pattern. The expected frequencies come from that proposed pattern, rather than from crossing the margins of several sampled groups.',
'risk':'A study seeks to quantify the association between exposure or treatment and a clinical event. Whether participants are followed from exposure to outcome or sampled based on disease status determines which population risks can be estimated directly from the observed denominators.',
'nnt':'A clinical report compares adverse-event risks in an active or exposed group and a reference group over the same follow-up. A relative change and an absolute change describe different features, while NNT or NNH translates the absolute change into a number of people.',
'odds':'A two-by-two study table records exposure and disease status. The sampling design and comparison direction determine how odds are labeled. Reversing only one set of labels changes the direction of the reported ratio even though the same underlying association remains in the table.',
'rcode':'An analyst supplies a statistical calculation or output for a biomedical study. The task is to interpret the function, result or population comparison without writing code. Use the reported statistic, reference information, degrees of freedom and p value only for the question they address.'}
